1. STAR — Tell me about your project

S — Situation
Fossil and dinosaur occurrence data is available through sources like the Paleobiology Database, but it is not presented in a simple, interactive way for users to explore dinosaur fossil locations around the world.

T — Task
The goal was to build a full-stack web application where users can explore dinosaur fossil occurrences on an interactive world map, filter them by geological period, and view detailed information about individual occurrences.

A — Action
I built the frontend using React and an interactive world map. I created a Node.js and Express backend with separate routes, controllers, and Mongoose models. I integrated the Paleobiology Database API to fetch real fossil occurrence data, transformed the API response into my application's schema, and stored it in MongoDB Atlas. I used PBDB's occurrence ID as a unique identifier and implemented bulk upserts so repeated imports don't create duplicate records. I also integrated Wikimedia Commons for dinosaur images and implemented logic to prefer life-restoration images while keeping image enrichment separate from the core data import.

R — Result
The application can now fetch real-world fossil occurrence data, store it in MongoDB, expose it through a REST API, and visualize the locations on an interactive world map. The data pipeline is also designed to scale because repeated imports update existing occurrences instead of deleting and recreating the dataset.

2. PREP — Explain technical decisions
We'll use this whenever the interviewer asks "Why did you use X?"

Why MongoDB?
P — Point
I chose MongoDB to store the dinosaur occurrence data.

R — Reason
The data comes from an external API and contains nested information such as location, coordinates, image metadata, and source information. MongoDB's document structure fits this type of data naturally.

E — Evidence
Instead of splitting information across multiple relational tables, we can store an occurrence as a single document containing its taxon, period, location, coordinates, formation, and source.

P — Point
So MongoDB simplified storing and retrieving the occurrence data while still supporting geospatial indexing for future location-based queries.


